  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nPrecipitation chances will decrease through the morning hours today \nas shortwave ridging builds eastward into the area. However, \nmoisture will be trapped in the lower portions of the atmosphere so \nwe'll continue to see cloud cover. This should keep highs in the 60s \nacross the area.\n\nFocus shifts to Monday afternoon through the overnight hours as a \nstrong shortwave trough moves east into the central US. A surface \nlow will deepen across Nebraska with a front trailing southward \nthrough Kansas. Very strong southerly low-level flow will lead to \nanomalously high precipitable water values, supporting potential for \nheavy rainfall. In the upper-levels, a leading jet max may help\ninitiate storms in the warm sector. But's a trailing, stronger \njet max will move across the area later in the day and into the \novernight hours. And that seems to be the more likely time frame\nfor severe storms across eastern Kansas into western Missouri. \nStorms seem likely to initiate along the cold front in central \nKansas and Oklahoma during the afternoon. These may be \nsupercells initially but these should develop into a line as \nthey move east late in the afternoon and evening, with damaging \nwinds being the most likely hazard. Favorable 0-3KM shear of \n30-40kts may also support embedded circulations within the line \nof storms. With 0-.5km helicity values pushing 200 m2/s2 can't \nrule out a tornado or two over eastern Kansas and adjacent areas\nof Missouri as the line of storms moves east during the evening\nand overnight hours. The Slight Risk area was expanded \nnortheastward on the latest Day 2 Convective Outlook, with much \nof western Missouri and eastern Kansas in a Marginal Risk. \n\nUnsettled weather will linger into Tuesday, though how unsettled it \nmay be is a little uncertain. The deterministic NAM is considerably \nslower with the upper wave and and actually has some potential for \nsevere weather Tuesday morning. The GFS is further east with \npotential storms east of our forecast area. With the upper low \nmoving over Kansas into Missouri through the day, we should still \nsee some potential for precipitation through the day Tuesday. \n\nTemperatures through the remainder of the forecast look cooler, \nthough still slightly above normal for this time of year, with highs \ngenerally in the low to mid 60s.",
